  <p><strong>Why does a bulk activewear order differ from the sample a buyer approved?</strong> A sample may be correct while one later decision is not: a different fabric lot arrives, a logo process changes, the pattern revision used for cutting is outdated, or the size chart never reaches the production team. A final inspection can reveal a mismatch, but it cannot reconstruct an approval that was never documented.</p>
  <p>This guide is for wholesale, private-label and OEM/ODM buyers who need to turn five familiar complaints into a traceable question. It is not a list of FITCLO incidents, laboratory results or guaranteed remedies. For the broader inspection sequence, see our <a href="/blogs/activewear-quality-inspection-checklist/">activewear quality inspection checklist</a>.</p>

  <h2>Before diagnosing: build one issue record</h2>
  <p>Keep one file for the approved sample identifier, dated tech-pack revision, pattern revision, bill of materials, color reference, size chart and tolerances, logo artwork, care instructions and purchase-order version. Add the affected carton or production-lot identifier, clear photographs and the number of units checked. A photo of one garment shows a symptom; it does not tell you how much of a lot is affected.</p>
  <p>Separate three decisions: <strong>containment</strong> (which units should be held), <strong>cause</strong> (what evidence explains the difference) and <strong>disposition</strong> (rework, replace, accept with agreement or another documented outcome). Agree who makes each decision. The <a href="/blogs/activewear-purchase-order-checklist/">purchase-order checklist</a> shows where these release responsibilities should be specified before bulk.</p>

  <h2>1. Bulk colors are inconsistent with the approved shade</h2>
  <p>Ask whether the difference is between the sample and bulk, between bulk lots, or only between garment panels or trims. Compare physical samples under the agreed viewing conditions with the approved color reference. Check fabric and trim lot identities; avoid deciding from phone photographs alone, where lighting and processing can change appearance.</p>
  <p>Visual shade approval and colorfastness are related but different questions. The <a href="https://members.aatcc.org/store/tm61/495/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AATCC TM61 description</a> addresses accelerated laundering colorfastness, while <a href="https://members.aatcc.org/store/tm15/482/messages" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TM15</a> addresses perspiration exposure. A qualified laboratory and buyer must choose the applicable method, specimen, acceptance grade and colorways; neither method by itself proves two batches match the same shade. Our <a href="/blogs/activewear-colorfastness-testing-guide/">colorfastness guide</a> explains the distinct wash, sweat and rubbing questions.</p>
  <p><strong>Release question:</strong> Which fabric and trim lots are affected, what was the signed color standard, and has the buyer accepted a physical bulk comparison? Hold the affected lot until those questions are answered.</p>

  <h2>2. A logo lifts, cracks or peels after washing</h2>
  <p>First identify the exact application: heat transfer, print, silicone treatment, embroidery or another method. Keep the approved artwork, placement and method specification. Then compare an unused bulk garment with a washed one using the actual care instructions and recorded wash conditions. A peeling transfer, cracked print and loose embroidery call for different corrections; “make the logo stronger” is not a usable production instruction.</p>
  <p>The <a href="https://www.aatcc.org/learn/online-test-method-training/laundering-module" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AATCC laundering-method overview</a> identifies established ways to assess garment appearance and dimensional changes after washing. A laundering procedure does not supply an automatic pass threshold for a particular logo. Agree the wash method, number of cycles, appearance criteria and sample selection with the supplier or testing partner before claiming durability.</p>
  <p><strong>Release question:</strong> Does the failed garment use the same artwork, material and application process as the approved reference, and does the agreed wash review reproduce the problem?</p>

  <h2>3. A leggings waistband rolls during wear</h2>
  <p>Recheck the complaint on the intended sizes and movements: walking, bending and squatting. Record whether the top edge folds, the whole band slides, or the front or back rise shifts. Those are different fit symptoms. Compare waistband height and angle, elastic or band construction, body-to-garment balance and the actual fabric with the approved sample. A heavier band is not automatically the right fix.</p>
  <p>Stretch and recovery can be investigated, but the method must match the fabric. For example, <a href="https://store.astm.org/d2594_d2594m-21.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ASTM D2594/D2594M</a> covers stretch properties of knitted fabrics within its stated scope; it is not a universal test for every compression knit or a substitute for on-body fitting. The dedicated <a href="/blogs/leggings-pilling-sheer-roll-down-quality-checklist/">legging roll-down guide</a> covers more causes and sample checks.</p>
  <p><strong>Release question:</strong> Is the problem limited to a size, fabric lot or construction version, and can the proposed change be approved on a new fit sample before the remaining units are made?</p>

  <h2>4. Bulk sizes drift from the measurement chart</h2>
  <p>Use the current points-of-measure diagram and agreed measurement method. Compare several garments from each relevant size and production lot, not just one piece measured in a different position. Record the actual value beside the target and tolerance for waist, hip, rise, inseam or other style-specific points. Check whether grading, pattern revision, cutting, sewing and finishing were aligned; do not infer the cause from a single out-of-tolerance number.</p>
  <p>If laundering may change dimensions, the <a href="https://members.aatcc.org/store/tm150/556/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AATCC TM150 overview</a> describes garment dimensional-change testing after home laundering and notes that the method may not suit certain stretch fabrics. Ask a qualified lab which procedure fits the actual garment. See our <a href="/blogs/activewear-sizing-and-grading-guide/">sizing and grading guide</a> for how to create the measurement reference in the first place.</p>
  <p><strong>Release question:</strong> Which sizes and lots exceed the agreed tolerance, and what does a documented remeasurement show after both parties use the same method?</p>

  <h2>5. The production garment is simply not the approved sample</h2>
  <p>Sometimes no single defect explains the problem: the sample had a different lining, waistband, trim, pocket, pad, label or packing method. Lay the approved sample and bulk garment side by side, then compare the latest BOM, pattern, artwork, color and packing revisions. Track each divergence to a written approval or an unapproved substitution. This is a version-control problem before it becomes an inspection problem.</p>
  <p>When a change is necessary, document the substitute, affected quantity, revised price or timing if relevant, and the new approval sample. Keep one final version as the production reference. Our <a href="/blogs/activewear-sample-approval-checklist/">sample approval guide</a> explains why comments and physical samples must stay connected.</p>
  <p><strong>Release question:</strong> Can every difference be linked to a buyer-approved revision? If not, agree a written disposition before shipping the affected units.</p>

  <h2>A practical buyer handoff that prevents repeat disputes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Give each approved sample and specification a matching style code and revision date.</li>
    <li>List the measurable checks, methods and tolerances that matter for that particular product.</li>
    <li>Record fabric, color and component lots when they can affect the approved result.</li>
    <li>Review an early production sample before the full lot is completed, where the order calls for it.</li>
    <li>Keep an issue log with photographs, measurements, affected quantities, owner and closure evidence.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>No generic checklist guarantees zero defects. The value is making a difference visible early enough to decide what to do, with the buyer and supplier looking at the same version of the product. For a new style, that starts before the PO is released; for a repeat style, it starts by confirming what—if anything—has changed since the last approved run.</p>
